00:00:34,486 --> 00:00:35,403
from from linux.

13
00:00:35,403 --> 00:00:38,537
And a lot of different ones that
you can use on the command line.

14
00:00:38,537 --> 00:00:41,380
Vi is the one that I'm
going to talk about here.

15
00:00:41,380 --> 00:00:43,498
It's one classic utility.

16
00:00:43,498 --> 00:00:45,048
It's been around since 1976.

17
00:00:45,048 --> 00:00:49,855
It was based on a what was called
the visual mode of an early text

18
00:00:49,855 --> 00:00:51,154
editor on UNIX.

19
00:00:51,154 --> 00:00:53,929
So that's where the vi comes
from in terms of the name.

20
00:00:53,929 --> 00:00:58,097
And a lot of times you'll see it
replaced now by vim or vi improved,

21
00:00:58,097 --> 00:01:02,935
which is just a version of the vi that has
a lot more features in terms of editing

22
00:01:02,935 --> 00:01:06,897
with plug ins for instance for
some additional capabilities.

23
00:01:06,897 --> 00:01:11,026
So you have a lot of different
ones to choose from.

24
00:01:11,026 --> 00:01:13,940
You don't necessarily need to use vi for
your editing, but

25
00:01:13,940 --> 00:01:17,040
I do suggest you get at least some
level of familiarity with vi.

26
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:20,411
And the main reason for that is really
because in my experience it's the one that

27
00:01:20,411 --> 00:01:21,988
you can count on always being there.

28
00:01:21,988 --> 00:01:24,807
So the example I gave before
of maybe you've got a full

29
00:01:24,807 --> 00:01:28,329
environment that normally would
boot up on your embedded system.

30
00:01:28,329 --> 00:01:31,987
But for whatever reason you're
getting stuck at the boot prompt and

31
00:01:31,987 --> 00:01:34,438
you aren't able to load
the full system yet.

32
00:01:34,438 --> 00:01:38,654
Most likely vi will be an editor
that's available in that scenario,

33
00:01:38,654 --> 00:01:41,719
it might be that other
editors are not available.

34
00:01:41,719 --> 00:01:45,807
And so having a little bit of knowledge
of vi will let you be you use

35
00:01:45,807 --> 00:01:49,315
the command line for
editing in that type of scenario.

36
00:01:49,315 --> 00:01:53,035
So in terms of opening a file with vi,

37
00:01:53,035 --> 00:01:57,475
you can just use vi plus
the name of the file or

38
00:01:57,475 --> 00:02:01,444
vim in case you're using vi improved.

39
00:02:01,444 --> 00:02:03,481
And then once you're into vi and

40
00:02:03,481 --> 00:02:07,636
interacting you can use what's
called normal mode for command.

41
00:02:07,636 --> 00:02:12,427
So if you hit the escape key, you'll be in
a mode that allows you to use the keyboard

42
00:02:12,427 --> 00:02:16,047
instead of for typing text for
typing commands to the editor.

43
00:02:16,047 --> 00:02:19,544
And so one of the things you would do
inside the editor is navigate around

44
00:02:19,544 --> 00:02:20,660
inside the document.

45
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:23,603
So you can use the h, j, k, and

46
00:02:23,603 --> 00:02:28,481
l keys to move left down up or
right within the file.

47
00:02:28,481 --> 00:02:32,120
And this might seem a little strange
at first, but once you get used to it

48
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:35,458
it's pretty powerful because you
don't have to lift your keys or

49
00:02:35,458 --> 00:02:38,567
your fingers off the keys in
order to move around in the file.

50
00:02:38,567 --> 00:02:43,219
So you can go into and out of editing
mode very quickly and you can also move

51
00:02:43,219 --> 00:02:47,506
around in the file pretty quickly
once you get the hang of using it.

52
00:02:47,506 --> 00:02:50,669
Then when you're ready to manipulate text,

53
00:02:50,669 --> 00:02:54,089
you can use i to go into insert mode and
type text.

54
00:02:54,089 --> 00:02:58,741
You can also use the delete key
to the d option to delete text.

55
00:02:58,741 --> 00:03:01,549
And when you're ready to exit and
save a file,

56
00:03:01,549 --> 00:03:05,442
you hit escape to make sure you're
in normal mode for commands.

57
00:03:05,442 --> 00:03:10,316
And then you can use colon w to
write the file and colon q to quit.

58
00:03:10,316 --> 00:03:13,601
So again, there's a lot more
you can do with the eye and

59
00:03:13,601 --> 00:03:15,756
there are other editors out there.

60
00:03:15,756 --> 00:03:18,421
I encourage you to look
through the links here and

61
00:03:18,421 --> 00:03:22,386
if you're not familiar with vi get
a little bit of familiarity with it and

62
00:03:22,386 --> 00:03:26,039
some level of comfort so
that you'll be able to use it when needed.

63
00:03:26,039 --> 00:03:31,914
So we've talked about interacting with
the command line via the terminal, but

64
00:03:31,914 --> 00:03:37,365
we haven't talked much about how
those commands actually get executed.

65
00:03:37,365 --> 00:03:40,722
So it turns out what's happening when
you're interacting with the terminal is

66
00:03:40,722 --> 00:03:42,737
there's what's called a shell interpreter.

67
00:03:42,737 --> 00:03:46,061
That's interpreting what you're
entering on the command line,

68
00:03:46,061 --> 00:03:49,387
passing that off to the appropriate
application for execution and

69
00:03:49,387 --> 00:03:51,954
returning the results back
to you on the terminal.

70
00:03:51,954 --> 00:03:56,662
And so in addition to the interactive
use that we've showed in the previous

71
00:03:56,662 --> 00:03:58,842
videos and and the current video.

72
00:03:58,842 --> 00:04:03,324
There also be the option to put
a list of commands in a text file and

73
00:04:03,324 --> 00:04:05,159
pass that off as a script.

74
00:04:05,159 --> 00:04:08,269
And the shell interpreter will be the same
thing that's going to be executing every

75
00:04:08,269 --> 00:04:10,107
one of those commands out
of the script for you.

76
00:04:10,107 --> 00:04:13,227
There are lots of different
shell interpreters out there and

77
00:04:13,227 --> 00:04:15,742
a lot of them have very
similar command support.

78
00:04:15,742 --> 00:04:19,312
The bourne shell was one of
the original shell interpreters and

79
00:04:19,312 --> 00:04:22,151
that's often you'll find
that abbreviated sh.

80
00:04:22,151 --> 00:04:25,440
It was released back in 1979.

81
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:26,970
Then there was ksh and

82
00:04:26,970 --> 00:04:31,570
csh and several other shell
interpreters that were developed.

83
00:04:31,570 --> 00:04:37,598
And each of those interpreters had some
unique features in terms of options for

84
00:04:37,598 --> 00:04:43,185
commands that you could use with
the shell and inside your shell script.

85
00:04:43,185 --> 00:04:48,996
And so what happened was bourne again
shell or bash shell was created.

86
00:04:48,996 --> 00:04:53,098
As a way to group all these useful
features together from different shell

87
00:04:53,098 --> 00:04:57,011
interpreters and add some extensions
to the original bourne shell.

88
00:04:57,011 --> 00:04:58,242
That was the 1979 version.

89
00:04:58,242 --> 00:05:02,385
So for most of the stuff that we talked
about in this class, we'll talk about

90
00:05:02,385 --> 00:05:07,011
either using the bourne shell, s, h or the
buorne again shell which will call bash.

91
00:05:07,011 --> 00:05:11,600
So a few more useful things that you
can do from the command line and

92
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:16,704
getting into some more advanced things
you can do from the command line.

93
00:05:16,704 --> 00:05:20,415
There's the find utility that
will let you locate files or

94
00:05:20,415 --> 00:05:24,895
directories based on things like
the file name or the type of the file.

95
00:05:24,895 --> 00:05:29,783
So here's an example of a command
that will find the any file or

96
00:05:29,783 --> 00:05:36,097
directory that's named a directory under
the given directory that we're in.

97
00:05:36,097 --> 00:05:40,463
And so find dot means look under
the dot in this case means the current

98
00:05:40,463 --> 00:05:42,083
directory that were in.

99
00:05:42,083 --> 00:05:43,375
Find dot means look for

100
00:05:43,375 --> 00:05:48,002
any file with your directory with name
a directory under the current directory.

101
00:05:48,002 --> 00:05:50,337
And so what you see spit out.

102
00:05:50,337 --> 00:05:54,651
If you run that command would be
a list of all the directories under

103
00:05:54,651 --> 00:05:58,035
the current directory that
are named a directory.

104
00:05:58,035 --> 00:06:02,182
And so because we did our copy from
a directory to a copy target in

105
00:06:02,182 --> 00:06:03,600
the previous video.

106
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:08,375
We'll find two matches of that here
when we search for a file by file name.

107
00:06:08,375 --> 00:06:12,642
Another thing you will find useful from
in terms of using the command line,

108
00:06:12,642 --> 00:06:16,855
would be searching inside the content
of the files for specific content.

109
00:06:16,855 --> 00:06:20,605
So if I have two different files,
textfile and textfile2,

110
00:06:20,605 --> 00:06:25,250
I use the cat option to concatenate out
and look at the content of the files.

111
00:06:25,250 --> 00:06:29,042
I can see one of them says
AESD awesome is awesome.

112
00:06:29,042 --> 00:06:31,150
The second one says, CU is awesome.

113
00:06:31,150 --> 00:06:36,540
So if I do a grep command and
I specify is awesome.

114
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:40,108
It's going to find both of those match in
both of those files is going to spit out

115
00:06:40,108 --> 00:06:42,879
those file names and
the corresponding matching lines.

116
00:06:42,879 --> 00:06:47,234
And in this case I even get some color
syntax highlighting to show me which part

117
00:06:47,234 --> 00:06:50,653
of the string match the corresponding
text in these photos.

118
00:06:50,653 --> 00:06:55,896
So one thing that you'll notice about
the grep command that I specified.

119
00:06:55,896 --> 00:07:02,158
So I used the astrostea as
a parameter to the direct command and

120
00:07:02,158 --> 00:07:07,587
what this at the astrostea
in this case is a wildcard.

121
00:07:07,587 --> 00:07:11,920
So the star is telling the shell that any

122
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:16,909
character in a name of
a file in this directory

123
00:07:16,909 --> 00:07:21,394
should be passed into the grep command.

124
00:07:21,394 --> 00:07:26,515
And so if I just say star,
what it's saying is that list any files in

125
00:07:26,515 --> 00:07:32,568
this directory and include that as one
of the parameters to the group command.

126
00:07:32,568 --> 00:07:35,865
I could have gotten more
specific with this,

127
00:07:35,865 --> 00:07:40,550
I could have said textfile star text,
which would have meant any

128
00:07:40,550 --> 00:07:45,079
file that started with the name
textfile and ended with text.

129
00:07:45,079 --> 00:07:51,722
But by just specifying the star, it
means match any files in that directory.

130
00:07:51,722 --> 00:07:55,956
So in the case that you specify
a wild card on the command line.

131
00:07:55,956 --> 00:08:00,265
What the shell is going to do is figure
out what are all the different matches for

132
00:08:00,265 --> 00:08:04,793
that particular wildcard and then pass
that string into the ultimate command.

133
00:08:04,793 --> 00:08:09,274
And so the grep command in this case is
going to get past the string that looks

134
00:08:09,274 --> 00:08:12,078
like this is going to see
grep dash r is awesome.

135
00:08:12,078 --> 00:08:13,754
And then instead of seeing the star,

136
00:08:13,754 --> 00:08:16,037
it's going to see the expanded
copy from the shell.

137
00:08:16,037 --> 00:08:19,517
And so it's going to see a list of
all the different files that match.

138
00:08:19,517 --> 00:08:21,644
And in this case since
I specified the star,

139
00:08:21,644 --> 00:08:24,762
it would pick any of the files
that are in the current directory.

140
00:08:24,762 --> 00:08:29,480
So if I had a textfile, textfile 1234,
it would list all those

141
00:08:29,480 --> 00:08:34,045
on the output of the command and
pass all those in as parameters.

142
00:08:34,045 --> 00:08:36,109
So in addition to wildcards,

143
00:08:36,109 --> 00:08:40,492
one other useful tool that we can
use on command line are pipes.

144
00:08:40,492 --> 00:08:45,074
So the pipe character is a character that
will send the output of one command to

145
00:08:45,074 --> 00:08:46,920
the input of another command.

146
00:08:46,920 --> 00:08:48,337
And this is really useful.

147
00:08:48,337 --> 00:08:52,936
You see this used a lot in the next shell
scripting because it's very versatile.

148
00:08:52,936 --> 00:08:56,937
You can keep your applications
tailored to do one specific thing.

149
00:08:56,937 --> 00:09:01,310
And when you're ready to combine the
features of two different applications.

150
00:09:01,310 --> 00:09:05,763
You can just use the output of one fed
into the input of another command and

151
00:09:05,763 --> 00:09:08,763
pass that to a third executed
well if you want to.

152
00:09:08,763 --> 00:09:12,578
So it allows us to change commands
together based on standard input,

153
00:09:12,578 --> 00:09:13,639
standard output.

154
00:09:13,639 --> 00:09:17,868
So the same kind of thing that you'd use
if you're writing an application and

155
00:09:17,868 --> 00:09:19,830
using print f for standard output.

156
00:09:19,830 --> 00:09:23,226
You could be passing that as input to
a new command using the pipe character.

157
00:09:23,226 --> 00:09:29,490
So if I use a cat and a command and
cat out my textfile.text.

158
00:09:29,490 --> 00:09:34,092
Normally that would print the entire
content of textfiles text out to

159
00:09:34,092 --> 00:09:35,132
the terminal.

160
00:09:35,132 --> 00:09:38,092
If I use the pipe option and
send it to grep,

161
00:09:38,092 --> 00:09:42,969
then that's going to pass the textfile
text as the input to the command.

162
00:09:42,969 --> 00:09:47,353
So before we were specifying we want
the grep to read specific files.

163
00:09:47,353 --> 00:09:51,696
And in this case grep is just going to
read what we passed into it from standard

164
00:09:51,696 --> 00:09:55,286
out which comes from
the concatenation of textfile.text.

165
00:09:55,286 --> 00:09:59,516
So you can see if I do textfile.text
by pipe grep is awesome.

166
00:09:59,516 --> 00:10:04,380
I will get the same output that I would
get if I grepped the textfile.text

167
00:10:04,380 --> 00:10:07,742
file and
I get the match on is awesome characters.

168
00:10:07,742 --> 00:10:11,361
So in addition to wildcards and
redirection,

169
00:10:11,361 --> 00:10:16,373
in addition to wildcards and
pipes, there's also redirection

170
00:10:16,373 --> 00:10:20,665
that's really useful that
we can use on the terminal.

171
00:10:20,665 --> 00:10:25,998
And so redirection is going to
say take the output stream.

172
00:10:25,998 --> 00:10:30,260
So for instance, standard out in this
case, what you would normally print out to

173
00:10:30,260 --> 00:10:34,471
the terminal with the print command and
instead direct that to somewhere else.

174
00:10:34,471 --> 00:10:38,784
So one really common place that you
direct this to would be to a file.

175
00:10:38,784 --> 00:10:42,127
So using an example of the grep command.

176
00:10:42,127 --> 00:10:45,015
So our grep is awesome
where we're looking for

177
00:10:45,015 --> 00:10:48,430
matches from any files in
the directory to the string.

178
00:10:48,430 --> 00:10:50,701
If we use the redirect character and

179
00:10:50,701 --> 00:10:53,664
redirect to our search
result that textfile.

180
00:10:53,664 --> 00:10:58,135
What we'll see is that what normally would
have been printed out on the terminal

181
00:10:58,135 --> 00:11:01,018
is not going to be inside
the search result textfile.

182
00:11:01,018 --> 00:11:05,641
So if I can search result text, I'll see
the content matching what would have been

183
00:11:05,641 --> 00:11:08,462
printed out on the terminal
from my grep command.

184
00:11:08,462 --> 00:11:11,241
Similarly to redirection,
we can also append,

185
00:11:11,241 --> 00:11:15,463
it's just using two of these redirect
symbols one right after the other.

186
00:11:15,463 --> 00:11:19,491
So if I say echo hello into that
same search result textfile and

187
00:11:19,491 --> 00:11:22,826
then I cat out the contents
of search results text.

188
00:11:22,826 --> 00:11:25,740
I'll see that I've added
hello to the end of the file.

189
00:11:25,740 --> 00:11:29,254
Whereas if I would just use
one redirect symbol here,

190
00:11:29,254 --> 00:11:34,253
I would have overwritten the content of
the of the search result textfile and

191
00:11:34,253 --> 00:11:37,790
you wouldn't see these
initial lines here anymore.

192
00:11:37,790 --> 00:11:42,803
So the file permissions that control how
we can access and use specific files.

193
00:11:42,803 --> 00:11:47,956
There's a common way of handling this
in Linux systems and there's really

194
00:11:47,956 --> 00:11:52,960
three levels of permission for
user group and either world or everyone.

195
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:56,833
So permissions are broken up based
on who is the user of the file,

196
00:11:56,833 --> 00:11:59,721
typically the person who
has created the file.

197
00:11:59,721 --> 00:12:02,773
What is the group account that's
associated with the person that

198
00:12:02,773 --> 00:12:03,668
created the file?

199
00:12:03,668 --> 00:12:06,489
And then what are the permissions for
anyone on the user?

200
00:12:06,489 --> 00:12:10,028
Someone that's not the user
that created the file or

201
00:12:10,028 --> 00:12:14,782
in any group that's the main group for
the user that created the file.

202
00:12:14,782 --> 00:12:19,638
Ls is the command that we used before
to show the files in the directory.

203
00:12:19,638 --> 00:12:23,098
And you can use ls-l to show
details about permission info.

204
00:12:23,098 --> 00:12:27,826
So in this case,
if I'm looking at a list of files and

205
00:12:27,826 --> 00:12:32,876
directories that I've got in
a directory on my system,

206
00:12:32,876 --> 00:12:36,218
I can see these characters rwxr-x.

207
00:12:36,218 --> 00:12:42,147
What this is telling me is whether each of
the user or group or everyone has read,

208
00:12:42,147 --> 00:12:46,584
write or execute permissions
on the directory or the file.

209
00:12:46,584 --> 00:12:52,851
And so the the r means read the w
means right and the x means execute.

210
00:12:52,851 --> 00:12:57,801
So you can see who the user is
here from this first column that

211
00:12:57,801 --> 00:13:01,367
shows that the user is
my user account dan.

212
00:13:01,367 --> 00:13:03,248
And by default, the group for

213
00:13:03,248 --> 00:13:07,697
your user would also be the same
group account based on your username.

214
00:13:07,697 --> 00:13:12,253
So both of these, what this means that
the person the the user name dan has read,

215
00:13:12,253 --> 00:13:15,045
write, execute permissions
on the directory.

216
00:13:15,045 --> 00:13:19,266
And anyone in group dan has read and
execute permissions on the directory,

217
00:13:19,266 --> 00:13:22,750
which is the same as any user on
the system, would have read and

218
00:13:22,750 --> 00:13:25,036
execute permissions on the directory.

219
00:13:25,036 --> 00:13:31,098
So one thing that you notice
is when I just create a file.

220
00:13:31,098 --> 00:13:34,164
So in this case, like for
my textfiles here,

221
00:13:34,164 --> 00:13:37,405
I don't have execute
permissions by default.

222
00:13:37,405 --> 00:13:42,374
And this is to avoid a scenario where
you would be able to execute and

223
00:13:42,374 --> 00:13:44,949
have execute permissions on a file

224
00:13:44,949 --> 00:13:49,858
where you maybe didn't intend
to contain script instructions.

225
00:13:49,858 --> 00:13:53,676
So if you create hello
world example script file

226
00:13:53,676 --> 00:13:58,549
that just has a single echo
command that echoes hello world.

227
00:13:58,549 --> 00:14:04,477
If we do an ls-l on that file, you'll
notice that there's no executable bit,

228
00:14:04,477 --> 00:14:07,491
no execute permissions set by default.

229
00:14:07,491 --> 00:14:11,379
If I try to run that command
with this dot slash syntex that

230
00:14:11,379 --> 00:14:16,080
says execute this helloworld.sh
script in the current directory.

231
00:14:16,080 --> 00:14:20,783
I'll get a permission denied
message complaining that I don't

232
00:14:20,783 --> 00:14:23,141
have the execute permissions.

233
00:14:23,141 --> 00:14:25,736
So you can use chmod to
change the mode of the file.

234
00:14:25,736 --> 00:14:28,012
And if I do u+x, this means for

235
00:14:28,012 --> 00:14:32,930
the user account at execute
permissions and give it the file name.

236
00:14:32,930 --> 00:14:36,917
If I do an ls-l,
I can see a couple of things.

237
00:14:36,917 --> 00:14:38,447
One is that the color syntax changed.

238
00:14:38,447 --> 00:14:42,444
So I see that it's green now showing
me that I have execute permissions and

239
00:14:42,444 --> 00:14:45,874
I see this x bit set here in
the user section of the permissions.

240
00:14:45,874 --> 00:14:50,095
So now when I run the command I'll get the
hello world output that I would expect.

241
00:14:50,095 --> 00:14:55,296
So the permissions in addition to
specifying with something like u+x or

242
00:14:55,296 --> 00:14:59,913
some strings that you can see
detailed in the man page for chmod.

243
00:14:59,913 --> 00:15:03,960
You can also use octel
digits in numeric mode.

244
00:15:03,960 --> 00:15:09,878
And so If you add some combination
of 42 and one together.

245
00:15:09,878 --> 00:15:13,598
You'll get a setting for read, write and
execute for those three different

246
00:15:13,598 --> 00:15:17,392
permission groups that we talked about
already the user group and everyone.

247
00:15:17,392 --> 00:15:21,491
So if I do an ls-l on hello world and
I see that I have read, write,

248
00:15:21,491 --> 00:15:24,119
execute plus read and read permissions.

249
00:15:24,119 --> 00:15:29,333
If I do a chmod 766 what
the seven is going to need?

250
00:15:29,333 --> 00:15:32,332
Make the user account have read, write and

251
00:15:32,332 --> 00:15:36,096
execute permissions because
4 plus 2 plus 1 is 7.

252
00:15:36,096 --> 00:15:39,855
The 6 and
the 6 are going to mean set read and

253
00:15:39,855 --> 00:15:44,165
write permissions for
the group and everyone.

254
00:15:44,165 --> 00:15:48,505
And so
if I issue the chmod 766 Helloworld.sh.

255
00:15:48,505 --> 00:15:51,122
And then I checked my
permissions with ls-l.

256
00:15:51,122 --> 00:15:55,253
I'll see that I get the permissions I
expect here with read write, execute for

257
00:15:55,253 --> 00:15:57,770
user and read and write for
group and everyone.

258
00:15:57,770 --> 00:16:02,765
All right, so a really useful thing
that you can do with the next command

259
00:16:02,765 --> 00:16:07,679
line operation that will use for
embedded systems very often is you can

260
00:16:07,679 --> 00:16:11,527
use what's called secure
shell to get remote access.

261
00:16:11,527 --> 00:16:13,743
And you can use remote access for

262
00:16:13,743 --> 00:16:18,678
both interactively entering commands
to be run from the command line.

263
00:16:18,678 --> 00:16:22,603
And you can also use it for
transferring files with what's called scp.

264
00:16:22,603 --> 00:16:26,196
If you're using a virtual machine here,
you can set this up pretty easily with

265
00:16:26,196 --> 00:16:29,688
port forwarding rules and there's a link
here that tells you how to do this.

266
00:16:29,688 --> 00:16:34,203
But what you're basically setting here
is the IP address that you'll be able

267
00:16:34,203 --> 00:16:36,435
to reach it from in your host machine.

268
00:16:36,435 --> 00:16:40,735
And the port that will
redirect to port 22 which is

269
00:16:40,735 --> 00:16:44,841
the typical port used for
s is h on Linux systems.

270
00:16:44,841 --> 00:16:51,453
So if I'm from my window system, I'll be
able to access the command line terminal.

271
00:16:51,453 --> 00:16:54,630
If I use ssh-p giving and
get the port option and

272
00:16:54,630 --> 00:16:57,897
specifying 2222 which
is my host port here.

273
00:16:57,897 --> 00:17:00,883
Then I can specify my username and
this IP address,

274
00:17:00,883 --> 00:17:03,667
which is the one we enter here for
our host type.

275
00:17:03,667 --> 00:17:06,823
So this is an example for virtual box.

276
00:17:06,823 --> 00:17:11,400
If you have a physical Linux machine
that you're using, you just replace.

277
00:17:11,400 --> 00:17:14,305
If you had a port redirect
you to replace that here or

278
00:17:14,305 --> 00:17:17,967
you just remove the port if you're
using the standard ssh port.

279
00:17:17,967 --> 00:17:21,455
And you'd use the user account for
the system plus the IP address or

280
00:17:21,455 --> 00:17:24,330
the host name of the system
you're connected, okay?

281
00:17:24,330 --> 00:17:27,694
So if you are using windows or
windows environment for

282
00:17:27,694 --> 00:17:31,369
remote access to the command shell and
you want to use ssh.

283
00:17:31,369 --> 00:17:35,508
There's several different options,
two of them that I'll go over here.

284
00:17:35,508 --> 00:17:38,522
One is you can install
windows subsystem for Linux.

285
00:17:38,522 --> 00:17:41,354
This is available on newer versions
of windows, like Windows 10.

286
00:17:41,354 --> 00:17:46,171
You can also use msys and either one
of these environments will provide you

287
00:17:46,171 --> 00:17:49,831
access to ssh that you could
use from a windows machine.

288
00:17:49,831 --> 00:17:55,448
So if you want to transfer from a windows
machine to your virtual machine,

289
00:17:55,448 --> 00:18:01,263
you can use the scp command with a capital
P port option to specify the port.

290
00:18:01,263 --> 00:18:07,268
You can specify the source directory
here from the windows machine and

291
00:18:07,268 --> 00:18:12,153
the target, both username and
IP address with this colon

292
00:18:12,153 --> 00:18:16,443
tilda syntax meaning copy
to the home directory.

293
00:18:16,443 --> 00:18:21,060
The colon separates the machine
information from the directory and

294
00:18:21,060 --> 00:18:24,789
the tilde says copy to the home
directory for the user.

295
00:18:24,789 --> 00:18:29,815
So if I do that and
then I ssh into the virtual machine,

296
00:18:29,815 --> 00:18:33,743
what I'll see is that the if I do it ls-l.

297
00:18:33,743 --> 00:18:36,854
That some file that I transferred in is
going to be there in my home directory.

298
00:18:36,854 --> 00:18:41,103
Similarly, I could transfer out
from my virtual machine into

299
00:18:41,103 --> 00:18:44,793
the windows system with
this scp-capital P option.

300
00:18:44,793 --> 00:18:49,730
And then just swapping the order of source
and destination as arguments to scp.

301
00:18:49,730 --> 00:18:52,770
So this would transfer
from my home directory,

302
00:18:52,770 --> 00:18:57,790
a some file.textfile to my downloads
directory on windows in from vm.text.

303
00:18:57,790 --> 00:19:01,238
So you can use the same instructions and
ssh and

304
00:19:01,238 --> 00:19:05,403
scp to transfer to and
from from Lennox systems as well.